---
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const FirstProgram = `public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        System.out.println("Ready.");
    }
}`;

const FirstProgramOut = `Ready.`;

const MissingSemicolon = `public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        System.out.println("Ready.")
    }
}`;

const MissingSemicolonOut = `Main.java:5: error: ';' expected
        System.out.println("Ready.")
                                    ^
1 error`;

const TypeError = `int score = "seven";`;

const TypeErrorOut = `Main.java:5: error: incompatible types: String cannot be converted to int
        int score = "seven";
                    ^`;

const RuntimeError = `int[] scores = new int[3];
System.out.println(scores[3]);`;

const RuntimeErrorOut = `Exception in thread "main" java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException:
Index 3 out of bounds for length 3
	at Main.main(Main.java:6)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csa/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Setting Up and Running Java</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="what-you-need">
          <h2>What You Need</h2>

          <p>
            One thing: <b class="text-sn-theme">something that can compile and
            run Java</b> and show you what it prints. That is the entire
            requirement for all four units. Nothing in this course needs a
            graphics library, a database, or a package manager.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two pieces, if you are starting from nothing:
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b class="text-sn-theme">A JDK</b> &mdash; the Java compiler and
              runtime. Any current long-term-support release is fine;{" "}
              <a href="https://adoptium.net/" target="_blank">adoptium.net</a>{" "}
              gives you one with an installer for your operating system.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b class="text-sn-theme">VS Code</b>, from{" "}
              <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/" target="_blank"
                >code.visualstudio.com</a
              >, plus its <i>Extension Pack for Java</i>. Install the extension
              pack from the Extensions panel and it will find the JDK you just
              installed.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            If you already have Java running some other way &mdash; a different
            editor, a JDK that came with something else, a command line you like
            &mdash; keep it. Every program in this course is a single plain{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code=".java" lang="txt" inline /> file with no
            dependencies, so it runs anywhere Java runs, and nothing on these
            pages depends on which editor you used.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="first-program">
          <h2>Your First Program</h2>

          <p>
            Make a folder to keep this course's work in and open it in VS Code
            &mdash; <i>File &gt; Open Folder</i>. Inside it, make a file called{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="Main.java" lang="txt" inline /> and put this in
            it. Type it rather than pasting it &mdash; there is a reason, and it
            is the next section.
          </p>

          <Code code={FirstProgram} lang="java" frame="code" title="Main.java" />

          <p>
            Press the <b class="text-sn-theme">Run</b> button &mdash; the ▷ at
            the top right, or the <i>Run</i> link the extension puts just above{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="main" lang="java" inline />. A terminal panel
            opens at the bottom and shows:
          </p>

          <Code code={FirstProgramOut} lang="txt" frame="terminal" />

          <p>
            <b>The file name has to match the class name.</b> A class called{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="Main" lang="java" inline /> lives in a file
            called <CodeSnippet code="Main.java" lang="txt" inline />, capital
            M and all. This is a rule Java actually enforces, and it is the
            first thing that catches people arriving from Python or JavaScript.
          </p>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            <b>No Run button?</b> The Java extension pack is not installed, or
            it has not found a JDK &mdash; those are the only two reasons.
            Nothing here needs a project, a build file, or a{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="src" lang="txt" inline /> folder: one loose{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code=".java" lang="txt" inline /> file in an open
            folder is a complete, runnable program.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="three-errors">
          <h2>The Three Kinds of Wrong</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">This is the section worth reading twice.</b>{" "}
            If your last language was Python or JavaScript, Java fails at
            different times than you are used to, and the whole experience of
            writing it depends on recognizing which kind of failure you are
            looking at.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">1. It will not compile: a syntax error</h3>

          <p>
            The program is not valid Java, so it never runs at all. Drop one
            semicolon:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={MissingSemicolon}
            lang="java"
            frame="code"
            title="Main.java"
            mark={[5]}
          />

          <Code code={MissingSemicolonOut} lang="txt" frame="terminal" />

          <p>
            Note what you get: <b>a file, a line number, and a caret pointing at
            the character.</b> Compiler errors look hostile and are in fact the
            most helpful message in the language. Read the first one, fix it,
            recompile. Do not read all forty &mdash; one mistake often produces
            a cascade, and the first line is usually the only real one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">2. It will not compile: a type error</h3>

          <p>
            This is the category that does not exist in a dynamically typed
            language, and it is the reason Java feels strict at first:
          </p>

          <Code code={TypeError} lang="java" frame="code" />

          <Code code={TypeErrorOut} lang="txt" frame="terminal" />

          <p>
            In Python this line runs fine and blows up somewhere else, later,
            with a stack trace that points at the wrong place. In Java it is
            caught <i>before the program ever starts</i>. You will trade a
            slower start for far fewer mysteries at 11pm. That trade is most of
            what static typing buys you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">3. It compiled, then died: a run-time exception</h3>

          <p>
            The code was valid. It just asked for something impossible once it
            was running:
          </p>

          <Code code={RuntimeError} lang="java" frame="code" />

          <Code code={RuntimeErrorOut} lang="txt" frame="terminal" />

          <p>
            An array of length 3 has indexes 0, 1, and 2. There is no index 3.
            Read the exception name first &mdash;{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException" lang="java" inline />{" "}
            says exactly what happened &mdash; then read the line number.
          </p>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            There is a fourth kind, and it is the worst: the program compiles,
            runs, finishes, and prints the wrong answer. Nothing tells you.
            That one is on you to catch, and it is what the debug challenges in
            this course are for.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="brace-style">
          <h2>One Note on Style</h2>

          <p>
            Every code sample in this course puts an opening brace on its own
            line:
          </p>

          <Code code={FirstProgram} lang="java" frame="code" />

          <p>
            You will see Java written the other way, with the brace at the end
            of the previous line, and <b>both are correct</b>. Java does not
            care and neither does the exam. Pick one and be consistent; this
            course is consistent about this one so that the block structure is
            easy to see at a glance while you are still learning to read it.
          </p>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            If your editor reformats a file and moves the braces up a line, that
            is its formatter applying its own default &mdash; not a mistake, and
            not something you need to fight. It changes nothing about how the
            program runs.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="next">
          <h2>Next</h2>

          <p>
            If <CodeSnippet code="Ready." lang="txt" inline /> printed, you have
            everything you need for the whole course. Read{" "}
            <a href="./exam">The Exam, and How to Pace Yourself</a> before
            starting Unit 1 &mdash; it is short, and it will change how you use
            the rest of these pages.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
